This study used the publicly available “Casting Product Image Data for Quality Inspection” dataset [ 21 ], which represents real-world production conditions in submersible pump impeller manufacturing. The dataset comprises 7340 grayscale images captured in an operational foundry environment, reflecting authentic industrial constraints including ambient lighting variations, surface oxidation patterns, and handling marks. Each image represents a top-down view acquired at a quality inspection station positioned after fettling and grinding operations but before final machining. The imaging configuration employs monochrome sensors at  \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym}
				\usepackage{amsfonts}
				\usepackage{amssymb}
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$300\times 300$$\end{document}  pixel resolution, consistent with typical machine vision deployments where grayscale imaging provides adequate defect discrimination while reducing bandwidth and storage requirements relative to colour sensors [ 22 ]. The fixed overhead perspective ensures consistent part presentation across images. Figure  1  shows representative sample images from both classes. Fig. 1 Representative sample images from the casting dataset showing defective (top row) and non-defective (bottom row) pump impeller castings

Although the dataset encompasses seven morphologically distinct defect categories, the detection task is formulated at the binary level (defective/non-defective) for the following reasons. Bounding box annotations delineate the complete impeller casting boundary rather than individual defect sub-regions, consistent with the original labelling convention [ 21 ] and with industrial go/no-go inspection practice, where the actionable decision is part rejection rather than pixel-level defect segmentation. This part-level annotation approach does not artificially inflate mAP: a true positive requires the model to both localise the part correctly ( \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
