				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\textrm{IoU} \ge 0.5$$\end{document} ) and classify it accurately, so random or oversized predictions register as false positives.

Adopting the YOLO detection framework over a classification-only approach provides three practical advantages for industrial integration: (i) spatial coordinates for robotic rejection arm targeting; (ii) confidence scores for threshold-based quality tiering, allowing borderline parts to be routed to human review; and (iii) bounding box outputs that map directly to production line coordinate systems for MES integration. The limitation that this formulation does not discriminate between defect subtypes (e.g. blow holes vs. shrinkage cavities) is acknowledged; multi-class defect localisation with sub-region annotations is identified as a priority for future work. Detailed dataset characteristics are provided in Table  1 . Table 1 Dataset description Attribute Description Dataset name Casting Product Image Data for Quality Inspection Source Kaggle [ 21 ] Domain Industrial manufacturing—casting quality control Product type Submersible pump impellers Total images 7340 (Training: 6633; Test: 707) Classes 2—Defective: 4207 images (57.3%); Non-defective: 3133 images (42.7%) Image format Grayscale;  \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
